  "pipeline": [
    "UTF-8 text",
    "Language tag per word",
    "Normalizer (numbers, dates, units)",
    "G2P",
    "Duration + acoustic model (int8)",
    "Vocos decoder + iSTFT",
    "PCM 22.05 kHz → I2S speaker"
  ],
  "tech": [
    {
      "title": "Runtime",
      "text": "C99, no malloc: the caller passes one arena whose size is known up front. Int8 weights with per-channel scales, int8 activations with one scale per frame. Streaming keeps per-layer state, so every frame is computed once."
    },
    {
      "title": "On the chip",
      "text": "Int8 SIMD kernels for the ESP32-P4 (PIE) and the ESP32-S3 (128-bit SIMD), work split over both cores with FreeRTOS. Chip audio matches the reference C runtime on a PC."
    },
    {
      "title": "Models",
      "text": "A VITS teacher (~24M params) per voice, trained from scratch, is distilled into 0.8–1.8M-param students: duration predictor, acoustic model with pitch and energy, Vocos-style ConvNeXt decoder with an iSTFT head."
    },
    {
      "title": "Front-ends",
      "text": "One per language, in Python for training and in C for the device, tested to give identical token ids. Vietnamese: spelling-based G2P with 6 tones. Korean: Hangul decomposition and the standard pronunciation rules. Mandarin: pinyin readings with a polyphone phrase table and tones. Japanese: kana and kanji readings by longest match over a word table, numbers with counters. Brazilian Portuguese: spelling rules with stress, nasal vowels and the two r sounds, plus per-word Portuguese or English tagging. Spanish: spelling rules with stress, dialect-neutral. Indonesian: spelling rules with the two e's and stress. Hindi: Devanagari letters with schwa deletion and nasals. Latin-script pairs read a whole English sentence in English. English: CMUdict plus letter-to-sound rules learned from it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Data",
      "text": "Every voice is trained only on speech synthesized with VoxCPM2 (Apache-2.0) from a designed voice (about 98 h per Vietnamese + English voice, about 43,600 sentences for the Korean + English voice, 54,636 for the Chinese + English voice, 49,591 for the Japanese + English voice, 45,191 for the Portuguese + English voice, 45,857 for the Indonesian + English voice, 48,296 for the Hindi + English voice, 48,336 for the Spanish + English voice), with sentences chosen to cover rare syllables, numbers and code-switching."
    },
    {
      "title": "Tooling",
      "text": "PyTorch for training, ESP-IDF v6.1 for the firmware, PhoWhisper and Whisper for intelligibility, UTMOS and DNSMOS for naturalness and cleanliness."
    }
  ],
